            "text": "Makes a tensor of random samples from the UNIFORM distribution: any value between low and high, all equally likely.\n\nThis is plain noise in a range, with no value favoured over another - the same thing t.rand makes, but with the range as live inlets and the same interface as the other t.dist nodes, so you can swap a shaped distribution in and out of a chain.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.dist.uniform <dim 0: int> <dim 1: int> ...\nWhole numbers give the shape of each batch of samples; with none\nthe node sends one sample at a time.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.dist.uniform 64 64\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin (button):\nThe small button at the top left of the node. Click it, or send it anything, for a fresh tensor of samples. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\nshape (text, also an inlet):\nThe size of the tensor of samples, as whole numbers: [64, 64] is a 64 by 64 grid, [1000] a row of a thousand. Numbers typed after the node name set it (t.dist.uniform 64 64); with none it is [1], a single sample. A list or tensor of numbers, or a single number, sent into the inlet works too. A new shape does not send by itself - the next click uses it.\n\nlow (drag float, also an inlet):\nThe bottom of the range; samples can equal it. Default 0.\n\nhigh (drag float, also an inlet):\nThe top of the range; samples are always below it. It must be above low - a high at or below low is refused (a message in the console) and the node keeps its last range. That is why the demo's sliders do not overlap. Default 1.\n\nEach parameter is a drag float on the node and also an inlet. Setting one rebuilds the distribution but does NOT send; the next click samples from it. In the demo each slider goes through 't bang float', which passes the number to the parameter first and THEN clicks the node, so dragging a slider re-samples at once. A parameter that must be above 0 has a drag float that stops at 0.000001. A value the distribution cannot take (see each parameter) - 0 or below sent into such an inlet, say - prints a message in the console, and the node goes on sampling with the last parameters that worked.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrandom tensor:\nA float32 tensor on the cpu, in the shape set, filled with fresh independent samples on every click.\n\nTHE HISTOGRAM:\nt.histc divides the range from its min to its max into equal bins and counts how many of the 4096 samples land in each; the bar plot draws the counts, the first bin at the left. Its shape is the shape of the distribution. Samples outside min..max are not counted at all.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nload_bang draws the first 4096 samples when the page opens; click the node's own button for more. The heat_map (-1 dark, 3 bright) is even noise with no structure. The histogram is a flat-topped block from low to high, the bars jittering around the same height; outside it there is nothing. Drag low and high and the block widens (and lowers, as the same 4096 samples spread over more bins) or narrows (and rises).\n\nRELATED:\nt.uniform fills an existing tensor the same way. t.rand and np.rand are the plain uniform generators. Every other t.dist node shows a shape; this one shows the absence of one."
        },
